    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Create a short narrative based on a personal experience. Interactive: Provide feedback on short texts during a peer review session.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can create a narrative describing a lesson learned from a mistake (e.g., "I forgot my homework, so I will make a checklist.").<br>Can provide feedback on a peer's story about school experiences (e.g., "I like how you fixed the problem in your story.").<br>Can create a short paragraph on ways to improve study habits (e.g., "I will study every night.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing a picture of a student looking sad next to a messy desk with books and papers scattered. Students are asked to quickly write down two words that describe the picture or how the student might feel, linking to "mistakes" or "difficulty." Then, the teacher asks them to share one word orally. This activates prior knowledge from previous lessons on the theme "Learning from Mistakes."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a simple sentence on the board: "Yesterday, I forgot my book." (Past simple for mistakes). Then, adds: "Next time, I will make a checklist." (Future tense for improvement). The teacher models the writing process, thinking aloud about verb tense choice and vocabulary like 'forgot,' 'checklist,' 'improve.' The pronunciation of past tense verbs ending in "ed" is briefly reinforced as relevant, e.g., "I learned" /d/ vs. "I practiced" /t/ (if applicable to modeled sentence). The teacher also models reflecting on learning: "I learned a lot from my mistake."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I talking about something that happened in the future or the past when I say 'I forgot my book'?" (Expected answer: The past.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does 'Next time, I will make a checklist' mean I want to make the same mistake again?" (Expected answer: No, you want to improve.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning from Mistakes."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative excerpt (approx. 3-4 sentences) written on a projector or handout. The narrative describes a common school mistake, the lesson learned, and a plan for improvement, using past simple, future tense, and "should," along with target vocabulary (e.g., 'grades,' 'homework,' 'study habits,' 'improvement,' 'practice').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example text: "Last month, I received low grades on my math test. I didn't study enough. I felt bad, but I learned a lot from this mistake. Next time, I will plan my study time carefully and practice more exercises. I should improve my study habits to be more successful."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding written information to identify key details about personal experiences, challenges, and proposed solutions for improvement.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the example narrative individually.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What mistake did the student make?" (Students write their answer on a mini-whiteboard or a piece of paper).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The student will study less next time. (T/F)" "The student wants to improve their grades. (T/F)" (Students write T or F).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match the mistake with the improvement plan (teacher reads phrases, students point to relevant parts of the text).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences using past simple for experiences, future tense for improvements, and "should" for advice. They will integrate vocabulary such as: <i>mistakes, grades, study, habits, homework, feedback, progress, improvement, success, better, successful, challenging, carefully, well.</i> They will also implicitly practice the pronunciation of past tense verbs ending "ed" as they write them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences that have blanks. Students complete the sentences using the correct form of the verb (past simple or future tense) and a relevant vocabulary word from a provided list.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Last week, I _______ (forget) my _______. (homework)"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Next time, I _______ (try) to _______ (improve) my _______ (grades) by studying _______ (carefully)."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You _______ (should) _______ (practice) speaking English every day for better _______ (progress)."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Expansion):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides sentence starters related to academic challenges. Students expand these into full sentences, incorporating the target grammar and vocabulary. E.g., "I didn't finish..." (Student writes: "I didn't finish my project on time, so my grades were low.") "Next week, I will..." (Student writes: "Next week, I will plan my study habits better to avoid mistakes.") They are encouraged to use words like 'challenging,' 'successful,' 'well.'</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Feedback Preparation (Written):</b> Students write two sentences: one about a minor academic "mistake" they made recently and one about how they "will improve" or "should practice" to avoid it "next time." They then exchange their two sentences with a partner. Each partner writes one sentence of "feedback" using "You should..." or "I like how you used..." under their peer's sentences (e.g., "I like how you used 'carefully' to describe your studying." or "You should ask for help when the topic is challenging."). This activity introduces the concept of providing constructive written feedback.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning from Mistakes."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Individual short narrative composition followed by a structured peer review session (written feedback).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Individual Narrative Writing (7-8 min):</b> Students are instructed to write a short paragraph (3-5 sentences) based on the theme "Next Time, I Will Study Harder to Avoid..." about a personal academic mistake they experienced. They must describe:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The mistake (using past simple, e.g., "I forgot," "I didn't practice").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What they learned from it (e.g., "I learned a lot from this experience").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How they will improve next time (using future tense, "I will study more," "I will try harder," and "should" for advice to themselves, "I should improve my study habits").</li>
                        </ul>
                        They are encouraged to use the target vocabulary (e.g., 'grades,' 'homework,' 'challenging,' 'progress,' 'success,' 'better,' 'carefully,' 'well'). The teacher monitors and offers support, reminding students to focus on clear expression and appropriate tenses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Peer Review (Written Feedback) (5-6 min):</b> Students exchange their written narratives with a partner. Each student reads their partner's story and provides written feedback directly on the paper or on a separate note. The feedback should focus on two aspects:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One positive comment (e.g., "I like how you fixed the problem in your story." or "Your story about improving your grades is inspiring.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One suggestion for improvement (e.g., "You should add more details about how you felt." or "Perhaps you could use the word 'challenging' to describe the test.") using "You should..." or "Maybe you could..."</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher emphasizes providing constructive feedback respectfully, aligning with sociolinguistic competence.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">13-14 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students hand in their self-written narrative and the peer feedback they received/gave. The teacher will review these for completion and application of learning outcomes. The main focus is on the narrative writing skill.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer Check):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of the narrative: Does it clearly describe a mistake, a lesson learned, and a plan for improvement?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of grammar: Correct use of Past simple for the mistake, Future tense for improvement, and "should" for advice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inclusion of target vocabulary: Are at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>mistakes, grades, study, habits, improvement, successful</i>) used appropriately?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Constructive feedback: Did students provide one positive comment and one suggestion for improvement to their peer's story? (Sociolinguistic competence).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one challenging part of writing your story today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Write one sentence about a goal you will set for your 'study habits' next week." (Pragmatic: Setting goals for improvement).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you learn about writing about your experiences and giving feedback today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can the advice you gave or received help you in your own academic progress?" (Pragmatic: Reflecting on learning).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., Students actively engaged in writing their narratives and peer feedback. They applied past simple and future tense effectively.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Some students struggled with using specific adverbs like 'carefully' or 'well' in their descriptions; provide more sentence frames for adverb use. A brief review of minimal pair sounds (e.g., /ð/ in "they" versus /d/ in "day") could be integrated if pronunciation issues arise during verbal instruction or feedback, but the focus remains on writing.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on writing personal narratives about learning from mistakes. In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will synthesize the narratives they've written and the feedback they've received, potentially to create a summary or a presentation of their "improved study habits" project, making their learning visible and actionable for the 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>